TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Image 4"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"Launch choice relies on a 2021 VV18 example and the unbuilt Vega E; no performance/azimuth analysis for direct injection from 5.05°N into a 97.8° retrograde SSO, and Wikipedia is used as the launch database."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The statement that high altitude yields low resolution images “which is also desirable” is contradictory; the 600 km altitude is not derived from required swath, resolution or sensor field of view."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN is stated to indicate launch-facility location; RAAN depends on launch time/azimuth and orbital dynamics, not facility latitude alone. J2 precession rate is stated but not calculated."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory is a radio-astronomy facility, not an established EO downlink station; no link budget, data rate or data-volume assessment supports the claim that passes are sufficient. Access results cover only three days."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Five-year life is asserted without component/lifetime analysis. Drag-sail EOL is proposed without drag area, deployment reliability, orbital-lifetime, 25-year-rule or casualty-risk calculation; no propulsive manoeuvre is actually designed."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"References in tables [49]-[55] and [66]-[74] are absent from the bibliography; bibliography entries are bare URLs and include Wikipedia."}],"overall":"A readable preliminary design that addresses all mission areas with STK evidence, but it is weakened by missing software files, unquantified trade-offs, an unbuilt launch vehicle, inaccurate/contradictory statements and poor referencing. Adequate overall.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Mission purpose, Australian benefit, single-spacecraft rationale and likely users are described in detail with references. Operator is only implied (Australian facilities/government), and user/funding roles are somewhat vague, so low Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major design areas are covered with STK screenshots and an access table, but support is incomplete: no STK files, no quantitative swath/resolution, launch performance, link budget or drag-lifetime calculations. Launch selection is speculative and some orbit/EOL claims are inconsistent.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The most suitable orbit for this task would be a low-earth sun-synchronous orbit."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The most promising alternative is a ‘drag sail’"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions restate the main design choices and are linked to the body, but they are brief and partly generic. The claim of constant ground-station contact is inaccurate, and EOL conclusions are omitted.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Report structure and headings are clear, but presentation is not professional: figures/tables lack numbers and captions, references are URL-only and mismatched, Wikipedia is used, and several typos/contradictory statements remain.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Image 4","quote":"DN path for orbits 1-15"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Satellite-ZYZZsat-To-Facility-Mt_Pleasant_Radio_Observatory: Access Summary Report"}]}]}
